    "q-73213": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73213",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(3t+1){e}^{-t}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t+1){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(2-3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (2-3t){e}^{-t} & <0 \\\\ 2-3t & <0 \\\\ 3t & >2 \\\\ t & >\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
