    "q-73334": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73334",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v({e}^{-3/2}) & =2({e}^{-3/2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-3/2})+{e}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3/2}(-\\frac{3}{2})+{e}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-73334-a",
